Constructor chaining is a feature in Java that allows a constructor to call another constructor of the same class. This is useful when a class has multiple constructors that share common initialization logic. By chaining constructors, the common initialization logic can be factored out into a single constructor, making the code more concise and easier to maintain.

When a constructor is chained, the constructor being called is specified in the constructor declaration using the this keyword. The this keyword is a reference to the current instance of the class, and when used in a constructor, it refers to the constructor being called.

For example, consider the following class with two constructors:

```java
public class Person {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public Person(String name, int age) {
        this(name);
        this.age = age;
    }

    // getters and setters
}
```

In this example, the second constructor is chaining to the first constructor by calling it using the this keyword. This allows the second constructor to reuse the initialization logic of the first constructor, while also allowing it to set the age of the person.

Constructor chaining can be a powerful tool for simplifying and organizing code, but it should be used judiciously and only when it makes the code more readable and maintainable. It is also important to be aware of the potential for infinite recursion when chaining constructors, as this can lead to stack overflow errors.

