Constructor chaining in Java is a technique used to initialize an object using the constructor of another object of the same class. It allows a constructor to call another constructor of the same class as its first statement, passing arguments if necessary. This can be useful for creating complex objects with multiple components that require different initializations.

For example, consider a class `Person` with constructors for initializing a person with just a name, and another constructor for initializing a person with both a name and an age:

```java
public class Person {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

